# Untitled

Adsorption of carbon dioxide, methane and nitrogen on an ultramicroporous copper metal–organic framework
Xiaofei Wu a, Bin Yuan a, Zongbi Bao b, Shuguang Deng a,b,∗
a Chemical & Materials Engineering Department, New Mexico State University, Las Cruces, NM 88003, USA b Chemical and Biological Engineering Department, Zhejiang University, Hangzhou 310027, China
## A R T I C L E I N F O
Article history:  Received 3 March 2014  Accepted 14 May 2014  Available online 23 May 2014
Keywords:  Adsorption  Separation  Carbon dioxide  Methane  Nitrogen  Microwave-assisted synthesis  Cu-MOF
## A B S T R A C T
An ultramicroporous copper metal–organic framework (Cu-MOF), \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{hflfppb})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{hflfppb})_{0.5}\) \(\mathrm{[H_{2}h f l p b=4}\) , \(4^{\prime}\) -(hexafluoro-isopropylidene) bis(benzoic acid)] was successfully synthesized by a microwave-assisted method (1) with a shorter reaction time and higher MOFs yield. The obtained Cu-MOF sample was characterized with scanning electron microscopy for crystal structure, powder X-ray diffraction for phase structure, and carbon dioxide adsorption at \(273\,\mathrm{K}\) for pore textural properties. Single-component adsorption (adsorption equilibrium and kinetics) of \(C0_{2}\) , \(\mathrm{CH_{4}}\) , and \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) on 1 was measured using a Micromeritics ASAP 2020 adsorption porosimeter at 278, 298 and \(318\,\mathrm{K}\) , and pressures up to 1 bar. Isosteric heats of adsorption, Henry's constants, and diffusion time constants were calculated and carefully analyzed. Adsorption equilibrium selectivity \((\alpha)\) , adsorbent selection parameter for pressure swing adsorption processes (S), kinetic selectivity and combined separation selectivity \((\beta)\) for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) , \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}/\) \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) binary mixtures were estimated based on the single-component adsorption data. The relative high values of the adsorption selectivities suggest that Cu-MOF is a promising adsorbent for separating \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\) \(\mathrm{CH_{4}}\) , \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) gas pairs.
