## 1. Introduction
The greenhouse effect mainly caused by a rapidly increasing emission of carbon dioxide \(\left(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\right)\) and methane \(\left(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\right)\) has become one of the most serious issues and environmental concerns facing our civilization today [1]. A total of 6.1 billion metric tons of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) was emitted to the atmosphere by the United States in 2007, according to the Environmental Protection Agency [2], which leads to significant interest in removing \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) from the exhaust streams of fossil fuel combustion as the first step in carbon sequestration [3]. On the other hand, natural gas coming from landfills and biogas plants is about 50–85% \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\), with the remainder being mostly \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) (20–35%), \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) [4]. Therefore, an energy efficient process of separation of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\), \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) is of great importance for the wide utilization of natural gas in order to improve the purity, upgrade the energy content and also prevent the corrosion of equipment and pipelines caused by the acid \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) gas [5–7].Several techniques have been considered for gas separation. Chemical absorption can efficiently capture \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) from flue gas
Several techniques have been considered for gas separation. Chemical absorption can efficiently capture \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) from flue gas
streams, but the total cost (including regeneration and other operating and maintenance) is relative high [8]. Dai and co-workers reported the first successful production of mixed matrix asymmetric hollow fiber membranes for a variety of gas pairs separations, including \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) [2]. One problem with the membrane diffusion is the trade-off between selectivity and permeability [8]. The separation of \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) is carried out mostly by cryogenic distillation, which is highly energy and cost intensive, especially in small-scale application [9,10].
Compared to the above mentioned methods, adsorption-based methods including pressure-swing adsorption (PSA), vacuum swing adsorption (VSA), and temperature swing adsorption (TSA), may be one of the most promising approaches due to the inherent simplicity, ease of control, low cost, low energy demand and high regeneration rate [8,11]. As an important class of nanoporous material, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), have been found promising adsorbents for gas separation [12–19] and storage [20–26], due to the potential advantages such as well-characterized crystalline architectures, controlled pore size, record-high surface area, and desired chemical functionalities [27–31], compared to the traditional adsorbents.
The classical ways commonly employed in coordination chemistry are also used for MOFs synthesis at low temperature (below 100 °C). Otherwise, solvothermal syntheses are required
[28], which is the most widely-used technique. The primary problem with the traditional synthesis procedure is the relative long reaction time, from half day to several days [32–36].
Microwave method has been considered an efficient way for the synthesis of dense solids and nanoporous inorganic materials which normally require several days for their hydrothermal crystallization. Using this method, particle size distribution, phase selectivity and facile macroscopic morphology could be well controlled with short crystallization times [37–40]. In the recent years, microwave method has also been applied to the synthesis of MOFs. Morris and co-workers [41] reported that two new isostructural coordination polymers with novel anionic MOFs are synthesized under microwave conditions using an ionic liquid, [EMIm]Br (1-ethyl-3-methylimidazolium bromide), as the solvent and template, resulting in purer phase and higher crystallinity. Ni and Masel [42] reported on the assembly of three known MOFs, namely IRMOF1, IRMOF2, and IRMOF3, through a rapid microwave-assisted methodology. By manipulating the concentration of the reactant solution, the crystal size can be varied from micrometer down to submicrometer scale.
The very close molecular sizes of \( \mathsf{C O}_{2} \), \( \mathsf{C H}_{4} \) and \( \mathsf{N}_{2} \) (kinetic diameter is 3.3, 3.8 and \( 3.64\,\mathring{\mathsf{A}} \), respectively) make it relatively difficult for kinetic separation. Recent study showed that MOFs with ultramicropores \( (<7\,\mathring{\mathsf{A}}) \) have promising properties for separating gas molecules that are similar in size [43,44]. In this work, a copper-based metal–organic framework (Cu-MOF), \( \mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{h f i p b b}) \) \( (\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{h f i p b b})_{0.5}[\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{h f i p b b}=4,4^{\prime} \) -(hexafluoro-isopropylidene) bis(benzoic acid)], was chosen as adsorbent because it has small cages of \( (5.1\times5.1\,\mathring{\mathsf{A}}) \) that are accessible through smaller apertures of \( (3.5\times3.5\,\mathring{\mathsf{A}}) \) [45]. Cu-MOF was first synthesized by Pan and coworkers [45] with a 12-h solvothermal procedure. Previous work has already shown the successful kinetic separation of \( \mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{C H}_{4} \) on Cu-MOF, both in experimental and in computational way [14,46], but the selective adsorption of \( \mathsf{C O}_{2} \), \( \mathsf{C H}_{4} \) and \( \mathsf{N}_{2} \) system has not been studied yet, which inspires this research work. Here we report a fast microwave-assisted method to synthesize Cu-MOF (1) and the potential application of separating \( \mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{C H}_{4} \), \( \mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2} \), and \( \mathsf{C H}_{4}/\mathsf{N}_{2} \) pairs. Pure gas adsorption equilibria and intracrystalline diffusivities of \( \mathsf{C O}_{2} \), \( \mathsf{C H}_{4} \), and \( \mathsf{N}_{2} \) on this MOF sample were measured using a volumetric analyzer at temperatures ranging from 278 to \( 318\,\mathrm{K} \). Isosteric heats of adsorption of \( \mathsf{C O}_{2} \), \( \mathsf{C H}_{4} \), and \( \mathsf{N}_{2} \) were also calculated and carefully analyzed.
## 2. Experimental
2.1. Synthesis of \( \mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{h f i p b b})(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{h f i p b b})_{0.5} \)
The Cu-MOF was successfully synthesized by microwave-assisted method (1), based on the literature procedure [45]. All chemicals, \( \mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O} \) \( (99\%^{\ast} \), Sigma); \( 4{,}4^{\prime} \) -(hexafluoroisopropylidene) bis (benzoic acid) \( (\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{h f i p b b}) \) ( \( 98\% \), Aldrich), N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF) \( (99\% \), Aldrich), were used as received without further purification. \( \mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O} \) \( \left(0.384\,\mathrm{g},1.59\,\mathrm{mmol}\right) \), \( \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{f}\mathrm{i}\mathrm{p}\mathrm{b}\mathrm{b} \) \( \left(1.945\,\mathrm{g},\,4.96\,\mathrm{mmol}\right) \) and deionized water \( \mathrm{[80\,mL)} \) were mixed together, and evenly transferred to two \( 80{\-}\mathrm{mL} \) reaction vessels, with about \( 40\,\mathrm{mL} \) each. The reaction vessels were capped tightly and kept in the microwave reaction system (Multiwave 3000/synthos 3000, Anton Paar). Synthesis was carried out at \( 423\,\mathrm{K} \) with a heating rate of \( 5\,\mathrm{K/min} \). After the reaction under the autogenous pressure for only \( 90\;\mathrm{min} \), the samples were then removed from the reaction system and allowed to cool to the room temperature. The mother liquor was then carefully decanted from the product and replaced with fresh DMF to remove excess \( \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{f}\mathrm{i}\mathrm{p}\mathrm{b}\mathrm{b} \), followed by drying in air for \( 24\,\mathrm{h} \), blue product about \( 0.456\,\mathrm{g} \) was obtained.
The guest molecules incorporated in the crystals were removed under a dynamic vacuum at \( 473\,\mathrm{K} \) for \( 15\,\mathrm{h} \).
## 2.2. Material characterization
To characterize and analyze produced samples, powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD), scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images were employed. The XRD pattern was recorded using a Rigaku Miniflex-II X-ray diffractometer with Cu \( \mathsf{K}\alpha \) \( (\lambda=1.5406\,\mathring{\mathsf{A}}) \) radiation, \( 30\,\mathrm{kV}/15\,\mathrm{mA} \) current, and \( \mathsf{k}\upbeta \)-filter. A step scan with an increment of \( 0.02^{\circ}\,\mathrm{m}\,2\theta \) and a scan rate of \( 1^{\circ}/\mathrm{min} \) was employed to obtain the high-resolution patterns. Cu-MOF sample for SEM analysis was coated with a thin layer of gold using as putter coater. Also \( \mathsf{C O}_{2} \) adsorption and desorption isotherm at \( 273\,\mathrm{K} \) was employed to determine pore textural properties including the specific Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area, pore volume and pore size distribution by using a Micromeritics ASAP 2020 adsorption porosimeter.
## 2.3. Adsorption measurements
The adsorption isotherms of \( \mathsf{C O}_{2} \), \( \mathsf{C H}_{4} \), and \( \mathsf{N}_{2} \) at three temperatures (278, 298, and \( 318\,\mathrm{K} \)) and gas pressure up to \( 800\,\mathrm{mmHg} \) on 1 were measured volumetrically in the Micromeritics ASAP 2020 adsorption apparatus. All temperatures were achieved by using a Dewar with a circulating jacket connected to a thermostatic bath with a precision of \( \pm0.01\,\mathrm{K} \). About \( 0.4\,\mathrm{g} \) of 1 was used for the gas adsorption studies. The initial degassing process was carried out at \( 473\,\mathrm{K} \) for \( 15\,\mathrm{h} \) under a \( 0.0001\,\mathrm{mmHg} \) vacuum pressure. The helium gas was used to determine the free space of the system. The degas procedure was repeated on the same sample between measurements at \( 473\,\mathrm{K} \) for \( 6\,\mathrm{h} \). Ultrahigh purity grade \( \mathsf{C O}_{2} \), \( \mathsf{C H}_{4} \), \( \mathsf{N}_{2} \), and He from Matheson Co. were used as received.
When the adsorption equilibrium data were collected, the kinetic adsorption characteristics were measured at the same time by a previously described method [14–17]. At a given dose, the changes in gas pressure and adsorption volume as a function of time were recorded and converted into the uptake profiles, generating the adsorption kinetics, and the final adsorption amount at the terminal pressure determined the adsorption equilibrium amount at a given pressure.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Physical properties
Compared to the traditional hydrothermal method, the reaction time for microwave synthesis of Cu-MOF is greatly reduced, from \( 12\,\mathrm{h} \) to \( 100\,\mathrm{min} \). This is mainly because both nucleation step and crystal growth step are effectively accelerated by microwave irradiation [47]. Factors including rapid and more uniform heating, microwave superheating, creation of "Hot Spots", and increased rate of gel dissolution may explain the microwave effect on the synthesis process of nanoporous materials [37].
