In order to determine pore textural properties including the specific Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area, Langmuir surface area, pore volume and pore size distribution (Table 1), \(C O_{2}\) adsorption and desorption isotherm on 1 at 273 K (Fig. 3a) were measured in an ASAP-2020 adsorption apparatus (Micromeritics). As shown in Fig. 3b, the median pore diameter of 1 was found to be \(4.6\,\mathring{\mathsf{A}},\) which was correlated by the Horvath–Kawazoe model built in the ASAP-2020 software. We also employed the same method on 2, and slightly different results were obtained, with BET surface area \(80\,\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\) and \(\mathsf{H}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{K}\) median pore diameter \(3.5\,\mathring{\mathsf{A}}.\) This is probably due to the small particle size of 1 because smaller crystals may have higher external surface [49]. Compared to the previous published results (the median pore diameter of 2 was found to be about \(5\,\mathring{\mathsf{A}}\) by correlating the nitrogen desorption isotherm data at \(77\,\mathrm{K}\) with the \(\mathsf{H}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{K}\) model), [14] the median pore diameters obtained in this work better agree with the theoretical aperture width of \(3.5\,\mathring{\mathsf{A}}\) [45]. So for materials with relative small pore diameter, \(C0_{2}\) adsorption and desorption at \(273\,\mathrm{K}\) seems to be a better option to get accurate pore textural properties.
## 3.2. Adsorption isotherms
Fig. 4 gives the single-component adsorption and desorption isotherms of \(C0_{2}\) , \({\mathsf{C H}}_{4}\) , and \(\Nu_{2}\) on 1 at \(298~\mathrm{K}\) Neither gas reaches its saturation loading over the pressure range examined. The adsorption capacity of \(C0_{2}\) , \({\mathsf{C H}}_{4}\) , and \(\Nu_{2}\) at pressure around 1 bar is found to be 0.86, 0.47, and \(0.13\;\mathrm{mmol}/\mathrm{g},\) , respectively. All isotherms are of typical Langmuir type. Compared to \(C0_{2}\) and \({\mathsf{C H}}_{4}\) , the isotherm of \(\Nu_{2}\) is linear, showing the weak interaction between \(\Nu_{2}\) and Cu-MOF adsorbent. The adsorption and desorption branches almost overlap with each other, suggesting that the adsorption process is reversible and the adsorbed molecules can be fully recovered during the desorption process. The lack of hysteresis also indicates that relative fast adsorption and desorption kinetics are probably expected, which will be confirmed in the adsorption kinetic part.
The adsorption isotherms of \(C0_{2}\) , \({\mathsf{C H}}_{4}\) , and \(\Nu_{2}\) on 1 at three temperatures (278, 298 and \(318\,\mathrm{K})\) are plotted in Fig. 5a–c,
respectively. All these isotherms can be well correlated with the Langmuir isotherm model (Eq. (1)) using the equation parameters listed in Table 2 with high \( R^{2} \).
\[
q=a_{m}\frac{bP}{1+bP}
\]
where \( q \) is the amount adsorbed in mole per unit mass or volume, \( a_{m} \) is the maximum adsorbed concentration corresponding to complete monolayer coverage, parameter \( b \) is called the affinity constant or Langmuir constant and \( P \) is the pressure.
The product of the two Langmuir constants ( \( a_{m} \) and \( b \) ) generates the Henry's constant \( (K) \). The ratio of the Henry's constants of \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4} \), \( \mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{N}_{2} \), and \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2} \) yields the intrinsic thermodynamic selectivity \( \alpha \) on 1. The adsorbent selection parameter \( S \) defined in the following equation is found to be more useful in adsorbent evaluation and selection due to its combination of the ratio of adsorption capacity difference in components \( i \) and \( j \) for pressure swing adsorption process [15–17].
\[
S=\frac{\Delta q_i}{\Delta q_j}\alpha_{ij}
\]
where \( \Delta q_{i} \) and \( \Delta q_{j} \) are the working capacity that is calculated as the adsorption equilibrium capacity difference at adsorption pressure and desorption pressure for components \( i \) and \( j \), respectively. The adsorption and desorption pressures are assumed to be 100 and \( 1\;\mathrm{kPa} \), which is a typical operating condition for vacuum swing adsorption processes. Table 3 summarizes the \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4} \), \( \mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{N}_{2} \) and \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2} \) selectivities, \( \alpha \) and \( S \).
As shown in Table 3, 1 displays modest \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4} \) selectivity, with \( \alpha \) of 3.1 and 5 of 5.6, at \( 298~\mathrm{K} \), which is slightly lower than that of MOF-5 \( (\alpha=5.6) \) [17]. In the meantime, 1 also shows good \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2} \) and \( \mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{N}_{2} \) selectivity. At \( 298~\mathrm{K} \), \( \alpha \) value for \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2} \) is found to be 21.5, which is larger than that of MOF-5 (17.5) [17], but less than that of ZIF-78 (50) [27]. This result is also comparable to \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2} \) selectivity obtained from breakthrough experiments with equimolar mixtures (14 for ZIF-68, 24 for ZIF-69 [50]; and \( 18\pm1.7 \) for ZIF-95, \( 25\pm2.4 \) for ZIF-100 [51]). As a comparison, Table 4 lists the adsorption selectivities of \( \mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{N}_{2} \) on several well-known adsorbents, including MOFs [17,50,52], zeolite [53], active carbon [54,55], and mesoporous carbon [10]. It is observed that 1 shows \( \alpha \) value of 6.9 at \( 298~\mathrm{K} \), exceeding all the adsorbents listed. To the best of our knowledge, this is among the highest \( \mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{N}_{2} \)
selectivity reported so far. These results reveal the potential of 1 in separating \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{N}_{2} \) system, especially for \( \mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{N}_{2} \) pair.
As an important concept to measure the energetic heterogeneity of a solid surface and evaluate the potential adsorption processes for adsorbents, the isosteric heat of adsorption \( (Q_{st}) \) represents the strength of the interactions between adsorbate molecules and the adsorbent lattice atoms. \( Q_{st} \) can be calculated as following procedure [14–17]. As a given amount, the isosteric
heat of adsorption can be expressed by the Clausius–Clapeyron equation:
\[
Q_{st}=-RT^{2}\bigg(\frac{\partial\ln P}{\partial T}\bigg)n_{a}
\]
where \(Q_{s t}\,(\mathrm{kJ\,mol}^{-1})\) is the isosteric heat of adsorption, \(P\) is the pressure \(\left(\mathrm{kPa}\right)\) , \(T\) is the temperature (K), \(R\) is the gas constant, and \(n_{a}\) is the adsorption amount \(\mathrm{(mmol\,g^{-1})}\) . Integrating Eq. (3) as constant \(n_{a}\) , we can get:
\[
\ln P=\frac{Q_{st}}{RT}+C
\]
So under a certain \(n_{a}\) , we got different corresponding pressures at 278, 298, and \(318\;\mathrm{K}\) from adsorption equilibrium data, and \(Q_{s t}\) is then extracted from the slope of plot \(\mathrm{ln}P\) vs. \(1/T.\) As shown in Fig. 6, the isosteric heat of adsorption of \(C0_{2}\) almost keeps constant, while those of \({\mathsf{C H}}_{4}\) and \(\Nu_{2}\) increase with the adsorption loading, which may due to the intersorbate interactions that are promoted in the restricted pore space [56], indicating the energetic heterogeneity of the adsorbent surface.
In addition, the initial isosteric heat (the heat of adsorption at zero-coverage) can be calculated by van't Hoff equation (see Supporting information). The initial \(Q_{s t}\) for \(C0_{2}\) , \({\mathsf{C H}}_{4}\) , and \(\Nu_{2}\) on 1 is found to be \(28.3\;\mathrm{kJ/mol}\) , \(24.1\;\mathrm{kJ}\) mol, and \(19.8\ \mathrm{kJ/mol}\) , respectively. They are all in good agreement with the values obtained by extrapolation of the isosteric heat of adsorption curves to the zero loading. The \(Q_{s t}\) of \(C0_{2}\) is smaller than that obtained on carbon molecular sieve (CMS 3 K, \(38.9\,\mathrm{kJ/mol})\) [57] and on a zeolite \(13\times\) adsorbent \((43\,\mathrm{kJ/mol})\) [16]; the \(Q_{s t}\) of \({\mathsf{C H}}_{4}\) is also smaller than that of carbon molecular sieve (CMS 3 K, \(33.7\;\mathrm{kJ/mol})\) [57] but is
larger than that of zeolite \(13\times(18.3\;\mathrm{kJ/mol})\) [16]; while the \(Q_{s t}\) of \(\Nu_{2}\) is larger than that of engelhard titanium silicate (ETS-4, \({\sim}12\,\mathrm{kJ/mol})\) [58] and is very similar to that of mesoporous carbon \(({\sim}20\,\mathrm{kJ/mol})\) [10]. The higher heat of adsorption of \(C0_{2}\) suggests that \(C0_{2}\) has a relative stronger physisorption interaction with the Cu-MOF adsorbent than \({\mathsf{C H}}_{4}\) and \(\Nu_{2}\) do, which is expected in most adsorbents because of the significant quadrupole moment of \(C0_{2}\) .
## 3.2. Adsorption kinetics
where \( m_{\mathrm{t}}/m_{\infty} \) is the fractional adsorption uptake, \( D_{\mathrm{c}} \) \( (\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{s}) \) is the intracrystalline diffusivity of gas molecules in porous media, \( r_{\mathrm{c}} \) \( (\mathrm{m}) \) is the crystal radius, and \( t \) (s) is the time. This model assumes the mass transfer resistance for gas adsorption is dominated by the intracrystalline diffusion and the adsorbent crystals can be regarded as an approximately spherical object, which are reasonable on occasions where the kinetic diameter of gas is comparable with apertures. The ratio of diffusion time constant of \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) and \( \mathrm{CH}_{4} \) gives the \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4} \) kinetic selectivity. Diffusion activation energy then can be calculated by the temperature-dependent Arrhenius equation (see Supporting information) with the values of \( 23.8\;\mathrm{kJ/mol} \) for \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \), and \( 27.4\;\mathrm{kJ/mol} \) for \( \mathrm{CH}_{4} \). These values are much larger than those obtained on zeolite 13v- (1.8 and \( 5.7\;\mathrm{kJ}/ \) mol for \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) and \( \mathrm{CH}_{4} \), respectively) [16]. Diffusion activation energy for \( \mathrm{CH}_{4} \) is larger than that of \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) due to the different energy barriers for the molecule gases passing through the small windows with a comparable size.
If both adsorption equilibrium and kinetics are considered, a combined separation selectivity \( (\beta_{i,j}) \) can be defined as follows: [14,15]
\[
\beta_{i,j}=\alpha_{i,j}\sqrt{\frac{D_{c,i}}{D_{c,j}}}
\]
where \( \alpha_{i,j} \) is the intrinsic thermodynamic selectivity mentioned in previous part, and \( D_{c,i} \) and \( D_{c,j} \) are the diffusion time constants for component \( i \) and \( j \). Table 5 summarizes the diffusion time constants of \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) and \( \mathrm{CH}_{4} \) at pressure \( (\sim\!4\,\mathrm{kPa}) \) and three different temperatures on 1 along with the selectivities. An example is given in Fig. S3 in Supporting Information to show how to correlate the adsorption kinetic data shown in Fig. 7 with the diffusion models. Compared to the adsorption kinetic data of 2 \( (D_{\mathrm{c}}/r_{\mathrm{c}}^{2}) \) is \( 1.14\times10^{-4}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1} \) for \( \mathrm{CH}_{4} \), and \( 2.97\times10^{-3}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1} \) for \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \), at \( 298\;\mathrm{K} \) [14], both \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) and \( \mathrm{CH}_{4} \) diffuses much faster on 1 \( (D_{\mathrm{c}}/r_{\mathrm{c}}^{2} \) is \( 6.19\times10^{-4}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1} \) for \( \mathrm{CH}_{4} \), and \( 5.96\times10^{-3}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1} \) for \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \), at \( 298\;\mathrm{K} \) ). This is mainly because 1 has a larger median pore diameter. The \( \mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{CO}_{2} \) kinetic selectivity and \( \beta \) are found to be both 9.6 at \( 298\;\mathrm{K} \) on 1. It further confirms that pore size distribution is crucial to kinetic separate small gas molecules with close kinetic diameters. Considering effectively separating \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4} \), adsorbents with pore limiting diameter between 2 and \( 4\,\mathring{\mathsf{A}} \) are more promising based on Haldoupis's calculation work [59].
## 4. Conclusions
A microwave-assistant method was applied to synthesize Cu-MOF (1) with a much shorter reaction time (from \( 12\,\mathrm{h} \) to \( 100\;\mathrm{min} \)) and higher productivity, compared to the conventional hydrothermal method (2). The BET surface area and H–K median pore diameter were found to be \( 105\;\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g} \) and \( 4.6\,\mathring{\mathsf{A}} \), respectively. Adsorption equilibrium and kinetics of \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \), \( \mathrm{CH}_{4} \) and \( \mathrm{N}_{2} \) on 1 were determined experimentally, at three temperatures (278, 298 and \( 318\;\mathrm{K} \)) and pressure up to 1 bar. Isosteric heats of adsorption, Henry's constants, and diffusion time constants were calculated and carefully analyzed. Furthermore, adsorption equilibrium selectivity \( (\alpha) \), adsorbent selection parameter for pressure swing adsorption processes (S), kinetic selectivity and combined separation selectivity \( (\beta) \) for \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4} \), \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2} \) and \( \mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{N}_{2} \) binary mixtures were estimated based on the single-component adsorption data. Overall, 1 displays good selectivities for all three gas pairs (at \( 298\;\mathrm{K} \), \( \beta \) is 9.6 for \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4} \), \( \alpha \) is 21.5 and 6.9 for \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2} \) and \( \mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{N}_{2} \), respectively). In addition, \( \alpha \) value of 6.9 for \( \mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{N}_{2} \), to the best of our knowledge, is among the highest \( \mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{N}_{2} \) selectivity of all kinds of adsorbents reported so far. In conclusion, microwave-assistant method is promising for rapid and productive MOFs material synthesis and the obtained Cu-MOF adsorbent show great potential to separate \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4} \), \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2} \) and \( \mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{N}_{2} \) gas pairs.
